{"title":"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 12","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems\">2003 AMC 12B #12</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2003 AMC 10B Problems\">2003 AMC 10B #18</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Mods,_more_rigorous)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Mods, more rigorous)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>What is the largest integer that is a divisor of\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/9/c694dd7f6739034188fe758646f1fd28508df784.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(n+1)(n+3)(n+5)(n+7)(n+9)\\]\" width=\"278\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>for all positive even integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/b/02b90914276aa817ebcdcc763787a2348f9a93b8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text {(A) } 3 \\qquad \\text {(B) } 5 \\qquad \\text {(C) } 11 \\qquad \\text {(D) } 15 \\qquad \\text {(E) } 165$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"366\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For all consecutive odd integers, one of every five is a multiple of 5 and one of every three is a multiple of 3. The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/7/c1764cd0624246966ee9a84f13d3e1d2e205bfde.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\cdot 5 = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"73\" height=\"13\" />, so  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/8/30848fe2906a75e423084a915226d0b4e3105252.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)15}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"60\" height=\"28\" /> is the correct answer.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We'll just test all the answer choices.\n</p><p>Note that for any 3 consecutive odd integers, there will be exactly one multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/0/93032f1ab8b685d84a767a2fde0d9006a464b5e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3.$\" width=\"12\" height=\"12\" />\n</p><p>Let's list all possibilities of 3 consecutive odd integers. (multiple of 3, not multiple of 3, not multiple of 3), (not multiple of 3, multiple of 3, not multiple of 3) and (not multiple of 3, not multiple of 3, multiple of 3)\n</p><p>To support this further, list the first few  consecutive lists of 3 consecutive odd integers.\n</p><p>We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/d/abd9fe15d4b334b5a4c6f12415480f2d2a4855e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1, 3, 5), (3, 5, 7), (5, 7, 9), (7, 9, 11), (11, 13, 15), \\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"388\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>So if the first two consecutive odd numbers aren't multiples of three, the last one must be a multiple of three.\n</p><p>Therefore for five consecutive odd integers, there must be at least one multiple of three.\n</p><p>In the same fashion as we did above, note that for any 5 consecutive integers, there will also be exactly one multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/3/b835f2c6c592edc4583ce996f86bcc0d07ca8da5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, for any 5 consecutive odd integers, there must be exactly one multiple of five.\n</p><p>We can skip 7 since none of the answer choices are a multiple of 7.\n</p><p>Now we try <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/7/4e76ce9da0f428536412a4f539b127c608760686.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"21\" height=\"12\" /> 11 doesn't work since as we see the following set of 5 consecutive odd integers doesn't fit: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/f/bbfc32ae9e57cad145a94d00dbb5d404d07a6d05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(13, 15, 17, 19, 21).$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"144\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Since any 5 consecutive integers is divisible both by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, it also must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/8/30848fe2906a75e423084a915226d0b4e3105252.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)15}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"60\" height=\"28\" /> and no higher since we saw that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> does not work and that there is no answer choice that is multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/a/e0a0db32027a732ac57d37ef2ae9bb150f65b108.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>~mathboy282\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>For these types of questions, I like to test out two to three values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and look at the prime factorizations. I tried <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/6/ca6db8098edd1e9253b05ba9157e1dc4a7465ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"53\" height=\"12\" /> so that there would be no overlapping of numbers. When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/8/598a16d4b7dde3dec3a390109882d54bb0308ea5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 2$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/4/094b6e228db97e177b705a9aa6d4c44a10822017.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 * 5 * 7 * 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"99\" height=\"14\" /> which has a prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/4/ac4236309dbd31efdea10bdfe4bdd135583c7a8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3^3 * 5 * 7 * 9 * 11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"133\" height=\"17\" />. When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/6/ca6db8098edd1e9253b05ba9157e1dc4a7465ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"53\" height=\"12\" />, you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/6/476336260a739b6027dc08b389c346ded45d1942.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15 * 17 * 19 * 21 * 23$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"161\" height=\"14\" />. Because the problem states it must be valid for all positive even integers, whichever factor is shared between the two cases will be the answer. The prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/6/ca6db8098edd1e9253b05ba9157e1dc4a7465ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = 14$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"53\" height=\"12\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/8/d78be73cbe8e38b02a53bf393e73adbdd0ea4ff7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5 * 3^2 * 17 * 19 * 7 * 23$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"177\" height=\"17\" />. Notice that the common factor is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cde134f9e808d28ae10a44b9b8315be5026c015.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5*3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"14\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/9/f1965fae079a9ba2c0726c307070c2355dfcb213.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />. So, our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/8/30848fe2906a75e423084a915226d0b4e3105252.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)15}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"60\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>-N.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Mods.2C_more_rigorous.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Mods,_more_rigorous)\">Solution 4 (Mods, more rigorous)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Mods, more rigorous)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The answer choices give us three possible divisors to investigate: 3, 5, and 11; 15 and 165 are products of some of those three. Observe that 2, the common difference between the five factors, is coprime with 3, 5, and 11. This means that for any given residue for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />, as we have five factors, we are guaranteed that at least one of the residues modulo 3 and modulo 5 is 0, so at least one factor has residue 0 modulo 3 and modulo 5. (We can confirm this by a little casework, or we can see that even with a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/3/0e3efd9b14723a92c2ae891fe27780d5f8e2b215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> as a 'generator', it still will cycle within five times of adding 2). This guarantees 3 and 5 as factors. Yet, there are too many residues in modulo 11 for these factors to cycle through, so none of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/d/48db9af447e8511de0b45e63522827ac49dc1976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+1,n+3,\\dots,n+9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"177\" height=\"17\" /> is guaranteed to have a factor of 11. Thus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/8/30848fe2906a75e423084a915226d0b4e3105252.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${\\boxed{\\textbf{(D)15}}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"60\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>In fact, using this reasoning, we can show that any prime greater than 5 isn't a guaranteed factor. Furthermore, since these integers are all odd (alternatively, because the difference is 2, we don't run through all the residues modulo 2 or 4), none of them have factor 2, so we are sure that 15 is the largest possible value that is guaranteed - no answer choice analysis needed!\n</p><p>~LQS\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_WhyMath\">Video Solution by WhyMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by WhyMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/dQiw5lisd84\">https://youtu.be/dQiw5lisd84</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B\">2003 AMC 12B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 11\">Problem 11</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_13\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 13\">Problem 13</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/P